Hey [~accountid:61addb80b0b630006a695081]  - as mentioned on one of the GFT/Product-Rides syncs, there is a bit of follow up work to be sorted on this project relating to ‘card surcharges’. For a recap/overview of the issue, see the updated comments on this in the HQ memo (highlighted blue).

We need to figure out whether the country is like Germany (it matters whether the fee is _intended_ to be a card surcharge = considered to be relatively lower risk) or like the UK (it doesn’t matter what the intention is = riskier).

If this is not something that can be readily identified from self-research, you may need to sync with external counsel, in which case I suggest the question is asked to local counsel as follows:

{color:#bf2600}_“For context, Bolt operates in the country on the basis that passengers are charged by drivers. Bolt charges drivers a commission._{color}

{color:#bf2600}_Bolt is now looking to charge a service fee to all passengers directly each time that a journey is booked. However, all things considered, Bolt is unable to charge this fee on cash journeys (it’s complicated to collect this sum from drivers and ultimately not worth the complexity). As a result, Bolt is only intending to charge this fee on journeys where the passenger is paying by card. It will not be charged on cash journeys._{color}

{color:#bf2600}_Bolt is aware that this could be argued to be a ‘card surcharge’ even though that is absolutely not Bolt’s intention._ {color}

{color:#bf2600}_In some countries, such as Germany (a_ {color}{color:#bf2600}*_Type 1_*{color}{color:#bf2600} _country), we have identified this as being a low risk because the domestic prohibition on card surcharges takes into account the intention of the charge (so Bolt can defend itself on the basis that it would charge the same fee to all passengers if it was impractical). In other countries, such as the UK (a_ {color}{color:#bf2600}*_Type 2_*{color}{color:#bf2600} _country), it does not matter what the intention is, all that matters is whether a payment costs more - these countries are considered riskier._{color}

{color:#bf2600}_For the purposes of [country] should Bolt consider this risk to be that of a_ {color}{color:#bf2600}*_Type 1_*{color}{color:#bf2600} _country, or that of a_ {color}{color:#bf2600}*_Type 2_*{color}{color:#bf2600} _country?”_{color}

Ideally, you’d be get to the bottom of this by the end of next week as the central/local teams are hoping to implement this fee ASAP if the risk profile is considered acceptable. 

If the risk profile is ultimately considered acceptable and charging the fee is green-lit in the country, then the next step would be to consider any drafting that would help reduce these risks, but that is a consideration for later. 

Let me know how you get on - I’ve added a new question to the red flag check as Q.7 to fill in your response.

Separately, having looked at the red flag check, I think the answers for Q.1 are answers to Q.2? Could you please double check this? As a last comment, the final section also needs to be completed in due course. 

Many thanks 👌

